			<article>
				<p class="eyebrow">How-to guide</p>
				<h1>Pair an iPhone <span class="badge-soon">Coming soon</span></h1>
				<p class="lede">The Anywhere iPhone app shows your sessions, lets you answer agents that need you, and sends push notifications. It connects straight to the Mac over Tailscale.</p>
				<div class="callout warning">
					<p><span class="callout-title">Coming soon</span>The iPhone app isn't publicly available yet. This page describes how pairing works so you are ready when it is.</p>
				</div>

				<h2 id="before-you-start">Before you start</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>Tailscale runs on the Mac and on the iPhone, signed in to the same tailnet. pocketd refuses to pair without it.</li>
					<li>In Settings › <strong>Phone &amp; Remote</strong>, <strong>Reachable from</strong> is set to <strong>Tailnet</strong>, the default. The <strong>Tailscale</strong> row shows <strong>Connected</strong> and the Mac's Tailscale address. If it doesn't, click <strong>Set up Tailscale</strong>.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2 id="pair-the-phone">Pair the phone</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>On the Mac, open Settings with <kbd>⌘,</kbd> and select <strong>Phone &amp; Remote</strong>.</li>
					<li>Click <strong>Pair a phone</strong>. The <strong>Pair phone</strong> dialog shows a QR code and a code. Both expire after 5 minutes.</li>
					<li>On the iPhone, open the Anywhere app and tap <strong>Scan pairing code</strong>. Point the camera at the QR code.</li>
					<li>Confirm the pairing on the iPhone.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The Mac's dialog says <strong>Paired</strong> and closes. The phone shows in the <strong>Paired phones</strong> list.</p>

				<h3>Enter the code instead</h3>
				<ol>
					<li>On the iPhone, tap <strong>Enter code instead</strong>.</li>
					<li>In <strong>Mac address</strong>, enter the Mac's Tailscale address, such as <code>100.101.102.103</code>. If you leave out the port, the app adds 4517.</li>
					<li>In <strong>Code</strong>, enter the code that the Mac shows under the QR code.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>Five wrong codes in a row lock pairing for one minute.</p>

				<h3>Pair from a terminal</h3>
				<p>On the Mac, <code>pocketd pair</code> prints the same QR code, the address, and the code.</p>

				<h2 id="get-push-notifications">Get push notifications</h2>
				<p>The iPhone can get a notification when a session needs you, fails, or is done, and when an automation run ends. Turn each kind on or off in the app's notification settings.</p>
				<p>Notifications go from pocketd through the Anywhere push relay at <code>https://push.getanywhere.dev</code> to Apple. pocketd encrypts each alert so only your phone can read it. The relay stores no device tokens: the phone gives pocketd a sealed handle that only the relay can open. Live Activity updates are the exception: they pass through Apple unencrypted.</p>
				<p>To send pocketd's pushes through a relay you run yourself, see <a href="/docs/architecture#the-push-relay">The push relay</a>.</p>

				<h2 id="remove-a-phone">Remove a phone</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>On the Mac, open Settings › <strong>Phone &amp; Remote</strong>.</li>
					<li>Under <strong>Paired phones</strong>, click <strong>Revoke</strong> on the phone's row.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The phone disconnects at once and has to pair again.</p>

				<h2 id="change-the-port">Change the port</h2>
				<p>pocketd listens for phones on port 4517. Change it only if another app already uses 4517. In Settings › <strong>Phone &amp; Remote</strong>, click <strong>Show advanced settings</strong> and set <strong>Port</strong> to a number from 1024 to 65535.</p>
